"The patient underwent robot-assisted radical prostatectomy for clinically significant prostate cancer. Prognosis is influenced by several factors. Preoperative findings included a high preoperative PSA of 86 and a pathological stage of pT4b with extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and seminal vesicle invasion. Furthermore, lymph node metastasis was present. The biopsy revealed a higher grade, Gleason 4+4, compared to the surgical specimen's Gleason 4+3.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node metastasis in the surgical report, although nodal metastasis was documented. The presence of positive margins and seminal vesicle invasion are adverse findings. The nodal status was documented as positive. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy difference in Gleason grade is a noted discrepancy.\n\nInformation that would revise the prognosis significantly includes the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data currently widens the uncertainty regarding recurrence risk. The heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 29.84 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 23.87–35.81 months; its coverage has not been validated."
